From: EasyMesh Lab
Subject: [PATCH] nbapi: drain signal bursts with an event-loop fairness budget

One AP metrics response updates many station objects and can enqueue thousands
of Ambiorix notifications. Dispatching only one notification per epoll turn
allows continuous native reports to outrun the consumer. The 100-client
traffic regression accumulates 198686 pending events and over 100 MiB of RSS
in its measured window, with growth continuing after traffic stops.

Drain up to 256 notifications or two milliseconds per callback, retaining
the shared reader mutex. The existing level-triggered signal descriptor
remains readable when work remains. No notification is discarded, no native
reporting period changes, and the event loop regains control at the budget.

diff --git a/framework/platform/nbapi/ambiorix_impl.cpp b/framework/platform/nbapi/ambiorix_impl.cpp
--- a/framework/platform/nbapi/ambiorix_impl.cpp
+++ b/framework/platform/nbapi/ambiorix_impl.cpp
@@ -15,6 +15,7 @@
 #include <amxd/amxd_transaction.h>
 
 #include <bcl/network/network_utils.h>
+#include <chrono>
 #include <mapf/common/utils.h>
 #include <tlvf/tlvftypes.h>
 
@@ -218,7 +219,13 @@ bool AmbiorixImpl::init_signal_loop()
         .on_read =
             [&](int fd, EventLoop &loop) {
                 std::lock_guard<std::mutex> guard(amxp_signal_read_mutex);
-                amxp_signal_read();
+                const auto deadline = std::chrono::steady_clock::now() + std::chrono::milliseconds(2);
+                for (unsigned dispatched = 0; dispatched < 256; ++dispatched) {
+                    if (amxp_signal_read() != 0 ||
+                        std::chrono::steady_clock::now() >= deadline) {
+                        break;
+                    }
+                }
                 return true;
             },
         // Not implemented
